WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:03.620
PETE: I think one of the interesting things
you guys are doing here is related to fraud

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:05.580
prevention.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.289
How do you guys stop online fraud?

00:00:07.289 --> 00:00:08.289
VIDA: Right.

00:00:08.289 --> 00:00:11.460
Well, it's a really important issue for us
because anytime you're giving out something

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:17.690
for free, there's a possibility that fraud
is going to creep into the system and just

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.530
make your whole product kind of worthless
to your retailer.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:29.850
So, we looked at some point into using IP
addresses, knowing bad IP addresses from users,

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.940
looking at why browsers the kind of traditional
way to fight fraud.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.200
And then we realized we had such a rich set
of signals from the social data.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:44.309
So, we have from our first campaign from Skype
that we know so far, we launched a campaign

00:00:44.309 --> 00:00:48.540
there, where you could if you like you can
get a free Skype code.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.379
We found that our fraud raise were better
than industry average, basically because we

00:00:53.379 --> 00:00:58.460
have a social data to tell us whether the
user was fraudulent or not.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:03.520
So, if you think about it, it's really hard
for someone to have signed-up for a multiple,

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.489
it's so easy to sign-up for multiple email
accounts.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.320
I'm sure we all have several, the one that
don't you just give real people, those things.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.880
With the Facebook want is it becomes really
apparent which account is real and not real

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:19.250
because you have more friends on your real
account, you update it more often.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:24.979
We get all that data to know how often does
someone update their status, what is their

00:01:24.979 --> 00:01:31.770
profile picture, how many friends do they
have, such a rich set of data that even though,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.760
our algorithms we started I think they're
solid.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:41.670
I don't think we've, by any means, gone to
the limits of our social data fraud filtering

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.170
capability but because of signals are so strong,
we're actually able to get a really good signal.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.840
PETE: That's very interesting.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:53.880
Are you using aggregated social data from
different social nets or is it mostly Facebook

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.850
that has the biggest use to you right now?

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.880
VIDA: Facebook is the biggest use to us right
now because Facebook credits are the most

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.690
popular incentives that we're offering.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:04.690
PETE: I see.

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:08.770
VIDA: We have a Twitter product where you
can incentivize someone tweeting.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:13.310
We haven't actually used it for social data
fraud for fraud filtering right now.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:14.849
PETE: Got it, okay.

00:02:14.849 --> 00:02:21.379
So, if I'm an engineer and I'm building an
application and I want to stop online fraud,

00:02:21.379 --> 00:02:24.609
what's the first thing I should do?

00:02:24.609 --> 00:02:29.810
VIDA: I think engineers will tend to design
an algorithm for it - instead think of things

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:35.919
that you can do and natural signals out there
because your AI algorithms as crazy and complicated

00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:38.549
as they are only going to be as good as your
signals.

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:42.840
If Facebook is actually, Facebook Connect
is pretty decent, you can check if an account

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:43.999
is verified or not.

00:02:43.999 --> 00:02:50.340
It needs to be verified by a mobile phone
number rather than just an email account or

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:56.480
something that's easy, you know, everyone
has limited mobile phones.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:05.579
I would actually really even consider policy
things.

00:03:05.579 --> 00:03:07.680
That's my tendency, is to go with the practical.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.999
PETE: One of the interesting things that we
talked about before was when you mentioned

00:03:11.999 --> 00:03:16.201
that the online fraud doesn't always come
- It's not always a trickle.

00:03:16.201 --> 00:03:21.680
It often comes in sort of a blast like one
big storm.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.269
Explain that phenomenon and what that means.

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:30.650
VIDA: I think what happens, we actually had,
so what happened for a while was that there

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:35.689
was a number of people who figured out a credit
card number that worked on one of our retailer

00:03:35.689 --> 00:03:39.760
sites that wasn't a real credit card number
or maybe it was a stolen credit card number,

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.799
I don't even know.

00:03:41.799 --> 00:03:43.529
It just got out.

00:03:43.529 --> 00:03:46.480
They must have posted about it in a forum
or things like that.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.939
So, all of a sudden overnight we had tons
of fraud that came in one big burst because

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:55.519
what happens is one person figures out how
to fraud the system.

00:03:55.519 --> 00:03:56.519
What's the first thing they're going to do?

00:03:56.519 --> 00:03:59.019
They're going to spread the word to all their
friends.

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:01.700
Especially since what we do a lot is Facebook
credits.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:06.760
So, these are people who play games or used
to gifting their friend's things everyday

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.590
and they gift it with the ability to fraud.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:17.750
So, we'll basically get a big burst of fraud
in one night and have to figure out the, figure

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:22.049
out the signal that will get rid of this fraud
and then push it within 10 minutes.

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:25.039
So, I'm going to change to shut this down.

00:04:25.039 --> 00:04:30.659
The first thing you want to do is come up
with some kind of control or some kind of

00:04:30.659 --> 00:04:31.659
a velocity control.

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:35.750
So, you can realize when there's a potential
for fraud to alert yourself for it that it

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:39.830
could be happening because you're not realistically
monitoring these things every day.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.770
PETE: The velocity of some particular metric
or set of metrics that you're kind of watching

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:44.770
across time.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:45.770
VIDA: Right.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:50.300
A particular campaign just seems to be picking
up speed and it's just a little bit suspicious.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.440
So that's the first piece to code up which
is difficult.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.330
It's not straight forward how to calculate
that kind of thing.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.099
And then when you go in to it you can look
at the data and pretty quickly come up with

00:05:01.099 --> 00:05:02.539
some kind of signal.

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:06.430
I think that's actually easy part, is coming
up with some kind of signal when you look

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:12.909
at, as a person when you look at just five
or ten of these fraudulent users you can usually

00:05:12.909 --> 00:05:15.520
find some characteristics pretty quickly that
they all have in common.

